  <h2>What it covers</h2>

  <p>
    A clinic runs on a handful of workflows that all touch the same patient record. HealthCareMS
    implements them as one system:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Appointments and queueing</strong> — booking, confirmation, cancellation,
      rescheduling, walk-in queues, slot-conflict checks and live queue boards.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Consultations</strong> — diagnosis with ICD-10 coding, vitals and trends, waiting
      room, and online video consultation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>E-prescriptions</strong> — PDF generation with QR, drug search, and allergy checking
      against the patient's history.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pharmacy</strong> — medicine catalogue, stock batches on FIFO, expiry and low-stock
      alerts, bulk import and barcode support.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Labs</strong> — orders raised from a consultation, with summary PDFs back on the
      record.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Notifications</strong> — email, SMS, in-app, and scheduled reminders.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The decision worth explaining</h2>

  <p>
    A feature list like that is where most projects reach for microservices — a pharmacy service, a
    lab service, an appointments service. I deliberately did not.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      These workflows share one patient record and need consistency across it. Splitting them into
      services would have converted simple database transactions into distributed ones, and bought
      independent scaling that a clinic will never need.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Prescribing a drug has to check allergies against patient history, decrement pharmacy stock, and
    write to the consultation record. In one database that is a transaction. Across three services it
    is a saga, with compensating actions, partial-failure states, and a new class of bug in a system
    where the failure mode is a patient's medication record.
  </p>

  <p>
    So the shape is a <strong>well-layered monolith</strong>: Domain, Application and Infrastructure
    with dependencies pointing inward, the domain kept persistence-agnostic, and the API composing
    the rest. The boundaries are enforced in the codebase rather than over the network — which keeps
    the option of splitting a piece out later, if there is ever a real reason to.
  </p>

  <h2>Where real-time was worth the cost</h2>

  <p>
    Not everything can be request/response. SignalR carries the pieces that are genuinely live:
    queue boards that must update as patients are called, and consultation chat with attachments and
    read receipts. These are cases where polling would be both wasteful and visibly wrong to the
    person watching the screen.
  </p>

  <p>
    Anything that is <em>scheduled</em> rather than live goes to <strong>Hangfire</strong> —
    appointment reminders, for example. Background jobs and real-time push solve different problems,
    and conflating them is a common way to end up with neither working well.
  </p>

  <h2>Operational choices</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>JWT with access and refresh flow</strong>, plus roles, permissions and permission
      middleware — a clinic has genuinely distinct roles, and "admin or not" is not sufficient.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Serilog structured logging</strong>, so production questions are answered by querying
      logs rather than reading them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Health checks and OpenAPI</strong> exposed from the API, so the system is inspectable
      from outside.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Docker Compose</strong> for the full stack, so the project actually runs for someone
      who clones it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it is public</h2>

  <p>
    Most of my production work sits behind client agreements. HealthCareMS is the one substantial
    system where the code itself is available to read — so if you want to see how I structure a
    codebase, name things, and draw boundaries rather than take my word for it, this is the one to
    open.
  </p>
